1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,500
Everyone keeps talking about MCP servers.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:12,320
Claude has them, Cursor has them, ChatGPT has them, and honestly, the way that most people explain them makes them sound way more complicated than they actually are.

3
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:18,240
Now, an MCP server is just a program that gives an AI model a list of functions that's allowed to call.

4
00:00:18,414 --> 00:00:18,800
That's it.

5
00:00:19,086 --> 00:00:33,120
So in this video, I'm going to break down exactly how they work, then we're going to build one from scratch in Python, and I'm going to show you the part that almost every tutorial skips, which is actually putting it live and setting up real authentication and authorization.

6
00:00:33,454 --> 00:00:34,756
Stay tuned, let's dive in.

7
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:36,640
So, what is an MCP server?

8
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:38,788
Well, let me break this down as simply as possible.

9
00:00:39,054 --> 00:00:41,252
Now a language model on its own can't do anything.

10
00:00:41,454 --> 00:00:45,636
It can't read your files, it can't check your calendar, it can't even query your database.

11
00:00:45,934 --> 00:00:49,508
All it can do is simply take text and then give you text output.

12
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:53,284
So if you want a model to do something, you have to give it access to tools.

13
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:59,440
And a tool is really just a function that has a name, a description, and some parameters, so some input and some output.

14
00:00:59,566 --> 00:01:05,040
Now the model can read these list of tools and decide, okay, I need to call this tool or tool X, right?

15
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:10,880
Now, when your model wants to call a tool, really all it's going to do is output text that says what tool it wants to call.

16
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:17,840
Your code is going to execute that tool, and then you're going to send the result back to the model or the LLM where it will be able to process it.

17
00:01:17,966 --> 00:01:26,720
So the model never calls the tool, it simply requests to call it, your code calls it, gives it the result back, the model is always working with text.

18
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:34,800
Now, before MCP existed, there was a bit of a problem, and that's that every single application had to build that tool plumbing that we just talked about themselves.

19
00:01:34,926 --> 00:01:38,160
And it was different for every single model and for every single tool.

20
00:01:38,414 --> 00:01:44,640
So if you built a GitHub integration, for example, for Claude, it didn't work inside of Cursor, it didn't work inside of ChatGPT.

21
00:01:44,814 --> 00:01:49,700
So everyone was rewriting the same thing over and over again, and we kept getting more and more agents.

22
00:01:49,998 --> 00:01:52,800
So MCP was invented just as a standard for that.

23
00:01:53,006 --> 00:02:02,852
Now it stands for model context protocol, and it just means that you write tools one time as an MCP server, and then any app that speaks MCP can use those tools.

24
00:02:03,118 --> 00:02:07,680
So Claude, cursor, VS Code, ChatGPT, doesn't matter, any AI agent out there.

25
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:11,492
Now people like to refer to this as something like USB-C for AI tools.

26
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:13,520
And honestly, that's a pretty good analogy.

27
00:02:13,646 --> 00:02:17,028
It's just kind of one plug that works in pretty much every adapter.

28
00:02:17,198 --> 00:02:19,200
Now there's two sides here that you need to understand.

29
00:02:19,406 --> 00:02:22,640
Now the first is the MCP server, which is just your code.

30
00:02:22,862 --> 00:02:24,080
So it exposes the tools.

31
00:02:24,302 --> 00:02:27,920
Now the MCP client is the app that the model lives inside of.

32
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:35,840
So something like Claude Desktop or Cursor, your AI agent kind of acts as the client, and then the server is where you actually write and expose the tools.

33
00:02:36,014 --> 00:02:40,160
Now, when a client starts up, it simply asks the server, what tools do you have?

34
00:02:40,366 --> 00:02:46,080
The server then sends back a list with all of the names and the descriptions, and that list gets handed to the model.

35
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:50,560
Then when the model wants to call one of these tools, the client sends a request to the server.

36
00:02:50,734 --> 00:02:56,880
The server will simply run the tool or run the function and then send back the result, and all of this will be handled in JSON.

37
00:02:57,006 --> 00:03:02,800
So it'll kind of just go back and forth, say, hey, I want to call this tool, okay, call the tool, get the result, give it back, give it to the model.

38
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:03,600
Oh, that's the result.

39
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:04,800
Maybe I should call another tool.

40
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:05,600
You get the idea.

41
00:03:05,806 --> 00:03:08,400
And there's two main ways that an MCP server can run.

42
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,840
The first way is local.

43
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:19,520
And that just means that the client actually launches your server as a subprocess that's running on your machine and it talks to it directly over standard input and output or stdi.

44
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:26,560
Now, Claude Desktop literally will run something like Python, myserver.py, and it will just pipe JSON into it.

45
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,760
Now that's what maybe 90% of the tutorials out there show you.

46
00:03:29,934 --> 00:03:34,240
And it's great for personal stuff, but it doesn't work when other people need to access the server.

47
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:37,280
Now, the second way that you can run MCP servers is remotely.

48
00:03:37,390 --> 00:03:39,520
Now your server will run somewhere on the internet.

49
00:03:39,694 --> 00:03:43,200
So it has a URL and a client will connect to it over HTTP.

50
00:03:43,342 --> 00:03:45,600
Now, this is how pretty much every real product does it.

51
00:03:45,968 --> 00:03:50,688
For example, the GitHub MCP server, the Notion one, Stripe, they're all really just URLs.

52
00:03:50,768 --> 00:03:58,528
And this is what you want if you're building something for other people, or you want to use your tools from different machines like your phone, your laptop, your work machine, you get the idea.

53
00:03:58,798 --> 00:04:00,688
So basically, you have local and remote.

54
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:04,448
Remote's going to be the preferred option, especially if you're going to go into production.

55
00:04:04,622 --> 00:04:12,608
However, as soon as you have a remote MCP server, you need to be careful because you introduce a problem that your local version never would have had.

56
00:04:12,750 --> 00:04:14,688
Now that problem is access control.

57
00:04:14,928 --> 00:04:19,620
If your MCP server is on the internet, that means that anyone has the URL can call your tools.

58
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:21,168
So your tools do things, right?

59
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:24,288
They read data, they write data, maybe they delete something.

60
00:04:24,494 --> 00:04:27,648
So you need to answer three questions on every single request.

61
00:04:27,854 --> 00:04:28,608
Who is calling this?

62
00:04:28,814 --> 00:04:29,888
What are they allowed to do?

63
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:32,048
And who are they doing it on behalf of?

64
00:04:32,208 --> 00:04:35,648
These are questions that need to be answered if you're going to have a remote MCP server.

65
00:04:35,808 --> 00:04:39,364
Now, with that in mind, here's the part that really shocked me when I actually looked into this.

66
00:04:39,598 --> 00:04:45,728
Now, security audits this year found that about a quarter of public MCP servers have no authentication at all.

67
00:04:45,808 --> 00:04:49,860
And of the ones that do have something, most of them are using a static API key.

68
00:04:50,048 --> 00:04:54,208
So just one long-lived secret that's sitting in a config file that's shared by everyone.

69
00:04:54,318 --> 00:04:59,328
Now, only a tiny fraction of them actually use the proper authentication scheme, which is OAuth.

70
00:04:59,502 --> 00:05:02,368
Now you might be thinking, why is a static API key a problem?

71
00:05:02,528 --> 00:05:03,328
Well, think about it, right?

72
00:05:03,488 --> 00:05:08,836
If five different agents are hitting your server and they're all using the same key, you're not able to tell them apart.

73
00:05:08,974 --> 00:05:11,728
You can't see which user told which agent to do what.

74
00:05:11,982 --> 00:05:15,008
And if one of them gets compromised, you can't revoke that one.

75
00:05:15,214 --> 00:05:18,048
You have to kill the key and break all of the agents at once.

76
00:05:18,254 --> 00:05:22,436
And that's fine for a short project, but it's not fine for anything that's real and in production.

77
00:05:22,702 --> 00:05:31,488
So the MCP official specification actually has an answer for this, and it's the same thing that all serious applications have already been using, which is called OAuth.

78
00:05:31,888 --> 00:05:35,248
specifically they reference OAuth 2.1 with PKCE.

79
00:05:35,328 --> 00:05:43,648
Now, if that sounds scary, don't worry, because you're never going to build this thing yourself, but you should understand what it is because we're going to build it into the MCP server we build in just a minute.

80
00:05:43,808 --> 00:05:46,528
It's an important part to keep your MCP servers safe.

81
00:05:46,734 --> 00:05:50,596
Now here's how this flow works your MCP server never signs into anything.

82
00:05:50,688 --> 00:05:52,048
That's really the key thing to understand.

83
00:05:52,238 --> 00:06:00,208
When Claude or any MCP client tries to call your server without passing a valid token, your server just says 401 not authorized.

84
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:05,088
And oh, by the way, here's the URL of the place that you can go to actually sign in.

85
00:06:05,168 --> 00:06:09,636
Now that place there that the server is going to return is called an authorization server.

86
00:06:09,728 --> 00:06:12,448
So the agent or sorry, the client will go there.

87
00:06:12,622 --> 00:06:18,848
The user will then see a normal login screen and they can sign in with Google, email, or whatever you've consented with or whatever you've set up.

88
00:06:18,928 --> 00:06:24,528
They'll get a consent screen and then they're able to approve an action and sign in just like they do with any other application.

89
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:29,808
So basically, what happens from here is that the user is going to click allow or whatever the authorization server says.

90
00:06:29,968 --> 00:06:35,716
It's then going to take a token and it's going to hand that over to Claude or again to whatever agent you're using.

91
00:06:35,888 --> 00:06:43,008
And then that agent will be able to send that token with every single request to your MCP server, and that's going to identify who you are.

92
00:06:43,214 --> 00:06:48,288
So your server is going to check the signature of that token, make sure that it was meant for this specific server.

93
00:06:48,368 --> 00:06:51,008
It's going to check what permissions or scopes are inside.

94
00:06:51,182 --> 00:06:52,228
And then it's going to run the tool.

95
00:06:52,526 --> 00:06:57,860
Now, just by the way, to go through some terminology, the permissions that are inside of the token are called scopes.

96
00:06:58,126 --> 00:07:04,208
So you might have like a notes read scope or a notes write scope, or you might have, you know, some other types of scopes, whatever.

97
00:07:04,368 --> 00:07:10,532
And basically the tools in your server can say, hey, I need this scope or this permission in order to do this.

98
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:18,660
So this allows you to not only just authenticate a user, but also to control what that user is able to do and to check if they have the required permissions.

99
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:21,568
Now, this token will also tell you who the user is.

100
00:07:21,998 --> 00:07:35,248
So embedded inside of that token data, it will have the scopes, it will have the user ID, and it'll have a little bit more information, which means your server can actually act on behalf of a user so it can scale out and not just work for one individual entity.

101
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:45,088
And there's just one more piece that you should be aware of here, which is that your clients, your AI agents, need to register themselves with your authorization server before they're going to allow you to log in.

102
00:07:45,198 --> 00:07:48,608
And the spec has something called dynamic client registration for that.

103
00:07:48,878 --> 00:07:50,208
So they can just do it automatically.

104
00:07:50,288 --> 00:07:58,528
You don't have to set anything up here, but you should just be aware that effectively what's going to happen is your client is going to register with the authorization server, say, Hey, I want to sign in.

105
00:07:58,688 --> 00:08:12,356
Authorization server sends a URL to the user, they sign in, that then grants a token, it gives that to the client, the client then sends that to the server on every single request, and that identifies you, the user, and all of the scopes that you have with the MCP server.

106
00:08:12,528 --> 00:08:30,448
It's actually a really nice flow, and you could build all of this yourself, like the login page, the consent screen, you know, the token signing, the client registration, the refresh logic, or you can just plug in something that's already doing this, which I'm going to show you in a second, because there's a lot of great tools out there that handle all of the MCP config for you.

